---
// Site identity bar. Sits in normal flow under the boot transcript, becomes
// sticky once the viewport scrolls past it. The hover-swap easter egg on the
// tagline lives here.
---
<header class="hostbar" aria-label="Site header">
  <h1 class="wordmark">James Klein</h1>
  <p class="tagline">Builds what the situation calls for. Documents it for the next <span class="next-up"><span class="original">person</span><span class="alt" aria-hidden="true">agent</span>.</span></p>
</header>

<style>
  .hostbar {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    background: var(--bg);
    z-index: 10;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--fg-dim);
    padding: 0.5em 0;
    margin-bottom: 1.5em;
  }
  .wordmark {
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.25em;
    color: var(--fg-bright);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    margin: 0.25em 0 0;
  }
  /* Cheeky hover swap: "person" -> "agent". Instant display toggle so the period reflows
     tight against whichever word is showing. The period lives inside .next-up so hovering
     it triggers the swap (a sibling text node would create flicker as hit-testing alternates
     between the span and the bare period). When "agent" (5ch) replaces "person" (6ch), an
     extra 1ch of right-padding keeps the hitbox visually identical regardless of which
     word is displayed. */
  .alt { display: none; }
  .next-up {
    padding: 0.4em 0.55em;
    margin: -0.4em -0.55em;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) {
    .next-up { cursor: help; }
    .next-up:hover {
      padding-right: calc(0.55em + 1ch);
      margin-right: calc(-0.55em - 1ch);
    }
    .next-up:hover .original { display: none; }
    .next-up:hover .alt { display: inline; }
  }
</style>
